hi there Steve here Steve Kaufman  remember by the way if you like my  videos please subscribe and give me  likes and stuff apparently that helps to  give you know me better ratings on  YouTube today I want about talk about  stress in language learning why it's bad  and how we get rid of stress in language  learning  amongst other things let's stop testing  people and asking them questions so a  bit of background today first of all is  July 1st and that means that it is kin  of the day it's the national holiday in  Canada so I'm wearing my red golf shirt  because I'm going to we've got a shotgun  golf tournament at the golf club for  Canada Day and everybody's supposed to  wear something red patriotic whatever  golf in golf when you are tense  you can't play well mood effects golf  tremendously but mood also effects  language learning so you know stress you  can have positive stress like I played  in a golf tournament with my hockey  buddies old-timers hockey group and  there it's a good you're all having a  good time we're playing sort of a  scramble and I did very well I hit the  ball well if I'm playing with people  that I don't like and I don't really  enjoy being out there I don't do well if  you're relaxed if you're if I'm thinking  about my swing and what I should do and  where I positioned my weight and stuff I  can't hit the ball if I feel comfortable  and relaxed I do well the same is true  in language learning and this came to me  because I went to dinner with this and  my wife and I went to dinner with this  Chinese couple that we know here and so  he of course he's an immigrant to Canada  and he's been trying to learn English  without much success although I think he  understands more than he did I tried to  get him on link he doesn't like doing  that he says he can't listen he serve  basically turns off fine so he's going  he's been going to class three hours a  day five days a week and he says very  tired and when he comes home he's tired  and stuff but he thinks he's doing well  ba  in the last say they test them every two  weeks and it turns out that he's  actually according to the tests getting  worse so he feels very depressed about  his English learning and I wouldn't say  he's he thinks he's motivated but  actually you know I try to speak to him  in English and we basically listens and  pretty soon the conversation degenerates  into Chinese so he's not successful but  telling him every two weeks that he's  getting worse in other words forcing him  to take this test is not very good  because we can do well sometimes and not  do well other times so for example in  that restaurant as we're leaving there's  a large table with Iranians so I say you  know hold its authority and I say a few  things in Iranian to them and then they  come back to me and all sudden I realize  that my efforts at learning Turkish have  basically wiped out my Iranian day it  hasn't Farsi it hasn't wiped it out but  it's just that in the pressure of the  moment I couldn't remember anything  pressure I inflicted it on myself by  speaking to them but nevertheless the  pressure to produce I couldn't produce  so then one of them says oh you're  learning Turkish I speak Turkish what  can you say in Turkish again pressure to  produce I better than anything now I  inflicted that all upon myself and I  don't mind it doesn't bother me that I  couldn't produce because I am confident  that in time I will be able to speak  Turkish and when I go back to Persian  refresh myself on my many stories find  new material eventually you know I'm in  no hurry and I will get there I'm  confident it doesn't destroy my  confidence but for this person who's  trying to learn English who has to learn  English who at some level would like to  learn English because he has emigrated  to Canada telling him that he's no good  doesn't help and and part of that is  that the whole testing thing for example  in Canada English in the English  speaking school system and I remember  this from when I went to school kids are  tested all the time and at the end of 10  years of learning French this is the  French kid French  in the English language school system  and they're tested and some kids do well  on the test and yet at the end of ten  years they can't speak French so all of  that testing and all of that instruction  really doesn't do much see what has to  happen and if I look at my Chinese  friend there he's expecting the teacher  to teach him he's not prepared to listen  and read and discover the language  somehow he wants the teacher to teach  him and so the teachers trying to teach  him and is drilling him and is testing  him with very poor results what has to  happen is that that you know language  instruction per se is almost a misnomer  what should happen is that people are  told I'm not gonna if I'm gonna teach  you I'm not gonna teach you the language  I'm gonna help you I'm going to tell you  where there are resources interesting  content at your level when you're  curious about grammar you can look up  grammar for example I look up Turkish  verbs and there's a site called Turkish  explained or explain Turkish or  something like that and it has all kinds  of good information about Turkish about  things that I am interested in I am  curious about things that I haven't  noticed in the language and when I read  those things that I'm interested in I go  haha the light goes on I understand that  when I see those structures I know why  that works that way but it's not  something that teacher has tried to  teach to me before I'm ready to absorb  it it's something that I'm interested in  so I'm exploring the language so somehow  we have to get rid of the pressure the  idea that the teachers gonna teach the  language that whatever the teacher  imposes on you you have to do including  writing the tests and somehow and I'm  I'm not a classroom teacher so I don't  know how best to do this but somehow  turn it around you're here to explore  the language whatever you discover in  the language is fine if you listen to a  content item and you understand half of  it that's fine if you misunderstand it  that's fine if you're listening the  stuff that you like the voice and you  kind of don't really understand it  that's fine you try to communicate and  you don't do  well that's fine because we can do well  sometimes and not do a lot of times so  we might do well on the test and then  not do well on the test the main thing  is to continue to engage with the  language if you continue to engage with  the language but in a situation where  you are driving the process you are  interested you are finding things of  interest now obviously here the teacher  has to help them the learner find  content of interest more or less you  know things that they have a chance of  learning we go back to that chit hack  behind me whatever this Hungarian guy  about the state of flow something that's  difficult but there is a sense that we  can achieve it either because we're very  interested in the subject or because  like even in my case I've now found  these Turkish podcasts and I go through  them which I can go to the link browser  extension bring it into link as a lesson  I go through and look up the words I  fight my way through the text and when I  listen to it I don't understand it but I  like the man's voice I'm exposed to the  language and I'm plowing through you  know I have the feeling that this  engagement with the language is helping  me no one's gonna test me on what I  understood I'm just doing that and when  I feel like it I go back to the many  stories where I understand more but the  whole point is remove the stress the  stress to perform the trust stress to  remember anything the stress to get  anything right the stress to remember  rules of grammar none of that matters  what matters is to stay active in it to  enjoy it and to believe in the process  and if you do that eventually you will  improve but that does mean putting the  sort of call it the impetus or the  responsibility on the learner and that's  I'm sure very difficult to do but maybe  that's what divides successful language  learners from not so successful language  learners they learn without stress they  put may put some pressure on themselves  but it's not a pressure to perform it's  that pressure to stay with the task  that's what I had to say and this  afternoon we'll see if I'm stressed or  not stressed while playing golf  bye for now  you

